# Comments & Docs — Python + Vue

Write like a tired senior dev. Short, plain, only when useful. Applies to Python backend (FastAPI, Django, Flask, SQLAlchemy, Pydantic) and Vue frontend (Vue 3, `<script setup>`, Pinia, Composition API, TS or JS).

## Core rules

- Comment the **why**, not the **what**. Types and code show what.
- If a comment restates the code, delete it.
- One line beats three. If it needs a paragraph, it needs a doc page.
- No section banners, ASCII dividers, or decorative headers.
- No emojis unless already in the file.
- No changelog comments (`# updated on X`, `// fixed bug`). Git handles that.
- No references to process artifacts in code: no story IDs, ticket numbers, sprint names, backlog items, ADR numbers, PR numbers, RFC links, Jira/Linear/Asana/Notion keys, or Confluence pages. Code outlives the tracker.

## Banned phrasing

No "Note that...", "It's important to note", "This function is responsible for...", "Here we...", "Let's...", "As you can see", "basically", "essentially", "simply", "just", "comprehensive", "leverages", "utilizes".

## Tone

- Contractions are fine. Talk like a person.
- Direct over polite. "Cache busts on deploy" > "This function ensures the cache is invalidated upon deployment."
- Dry is okay. "Yes, this is ugly. The API requires it." is a fine comment.

## Python

- One-line docstring for functions/classes. Multi-line only when params have non-obvious constraints or side effects matter.
- Skip `:param x: the x` filler. Type hints already say it.
- No type info in docstrings when the signature has type hints.
- Pydantic models: skip `Field(description="...")` unless it powers OpenAPI docs the frontend reads.
- FastAPI routes: use `summary=` sparingly, only when the path isn't self-explanatory.
- Don't comment `# noqa` / `# type: ignore` without a one-line reason.

## Vue / TypeScript

- No JSDoc on components when props are typed with `defineProps<...>()`.
- No `@param` / `@returns` filler in composables — let TS types speak.
- `<script setup>` blocks: skip "// imports" / "// state" / "// methods" dividers.
- Template comments (`<!-- -->`) only for non-obvious conditional logic or accessibility notes.
- Pinia stores: don't comment getters/actions that match their name. Comment the weird ones.
- Don't restate `v-if` / `v-for` conditions in comments.

## Use freely

`TODO:`, `FIXME:`, `HACK:` — each with a one-line reason.

## Before / after

Python — bad:
```py
def get_user(user_id: int) -> User | None:
    """
    Get a user from the database.

    :param user_id: The ID of the user to get.
    :returns: The user object, or None if not found.
    """
    # Query the database for the user
    return db.query(User).filter(User.id == user_id).first()
```

Python — good:
```py
def get_user(user_id: int) -> User | None:
    # Excludes soft-deleted; use get_user_including_deleted for admin views.
    return db.query(User).filter(User.id == user_id, User.deleted_at.is_(None)).first()
```

FastAPI — bad:
```py
@router.post("/orders", response_model=OrderOut)
async def create_order(payload: OrderIn, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):
    """Creates a new order. Implements US-204."""
    ...
```

FastAPI — good:
```py
@router.post("/orders", response_model=OrderOut)
async def create_order(payload: OrderIn, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):
    # Idempotent on payload.client_ref — safe to retry from the SPA.
    ...
```

Vue — bad:
```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
// Props
interface Props {
  userId: number  // the user id
}
const props = defineProps<Props>()

// State
const loading = ref(false)  // loading state
const user = ref<User | null>(null)  // the user

// Fetch the user on mount
onMounted(async () => {
  loading.value = true
  user.value = await fetchUser(props.userId)
  loading.value = false
})
</script>
```

Vue — good:
```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
const props = defineProps<{ userId: number }>()

const loading = ref(false)
const user = ref<User | null>(null)

onMounted(async () => {
  loading.value = true
  // No try/finally — parent ErrorBoundary handles fetch failures.
  user.value = await fetchUser(props.userId)
  loading.value = false
})
</script>
```

Pinia — bad:
```ts
export const useCartStore = defineStore('cart', () => {
  // The items in the cart
  const items = ref<CartItem[]>([])

  // Add an item to the cart
  function add(item: CartItem) { items.value.push(item) }

  return { items, add }
})
```

Pinia — good:
```ts
export const useCartStore = defineStore('cart', () => {
  const items = ref<CartItem[]>([])

  function add(item: CartItem) { items.value.push(item) }

  // Server is source of truth; call syncCart() before checkout.
  return { items, add }
})
```
